### 5. Structured Data Comparison — Prefer Assignment-Time Fingerprint

For any future need to compare structured data, if the compared value is an object, array, list, hashtable, dictionary, or any other multi-field / multi-item data structure, the first preferred design is to create a stable fingerprint/hash at the time the data is assigned, created, captured, or saved.

This rule does not apply to simple scalar values such as a single string, number, boolean, enum, or timestamp.

Preferred design:

* When structured data is created or assigned, also create a stable comparison fingerprint/hash for that data.
* Store the fingerprint/hash together with the original data.
* Future comparison should prefer comparing fingerprint/hash values instead of repeatedly comparing the full object or array field-by-field.
* Keep the original object/array for debugging, display, evidence, and mismatch investigation.
* The fingerprint/hash must be based only on fields/items that are part of the intended comparison identity.
* Do not include volatile fields unless they are intentionally part of the comparison identity.

Volatile fields usually include:

```text
Timestamp
LogTimestamp
RawTimestamp
PID
temporary index
runtime-only file name
session-only values
random IDs
```

Required priority for structured data comparison:

```text
1. Preferred: fingerprint/hash created at assignment/capture/save time.
2. Acceptable: explicit field-by-field comparison when no fingerprint/hash was prepared.
3. Not acceptable for persistent/reloaded data: raw object reference comparison.
```

Important retrofit rule:

Do not retrofit a new fingerprint/hash layer into an active repair or refactor unless the user explicitly approves it.

If the current implementation already uses explicit field-by-field comparison and there are open fixes in progress, finish the current repair path first. Mark fingerprint/hash comparison as a future cleanup instead of changing architecture mid-repair.

